@*
    A row in one of the three change lists: the game's name, and one right-aligned cell holding what
    happened and when.

    A row, not a card. Three lists of five boxed cards was three columns of boxes on a page whose
    only other framed thing is a connect screen — and a card per game gives a name, an age and at
    most six words the furniture of a section. Name left, age right, a hairline between: the shape
    the handoff draws, and the shape a list of "what changed" wants.

    **No explanatory suffix under the row.** It carried "first seen", "answered again", "we keep
    knocking" and, on the discovery feed, a mono line naming the referral that found the game and
    the codebase it answered with. The column heading above already says which register this is, so
    every one of those was the same fact announced twice — twenty extra lines for a screen reader to
    wade through on the front page. Where a *cause* exists it is not a repetition of the heading and
    it is kept, folded into the one right-hand cell beside the age: "refused · 16h" rather than a
    second line saying "connection refused".

    The return no longer raises its voice with a tinted card and a glow. It is the same row as the
    other two lists, with the accent on the cell that says it is back — one shape for one kind of
    thing, which is what lets the eye run down three columns at once.
*@
<article class="feed-row @Register">
    @* Off when the row sits under a column that already names the register, so the page does not
       read "newly discovered newly discovered". A screen reader still gets it: the columns are
       sections labelled by their own heading. *@
    @if (ShowRegister)
    {
        <div class="kicker">@Heading</div>
    }
    <a class="feed-name" href="/g/@Entry.Slug"><GameName Name="@Entry.Name" /></a>
    <span class="feed-state mono">
        @if (Kind is FeedKind.CameBack)
        {
            <span class="state-present">live</span>
            <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
        }
        else if (Cause is { Length: > 0 } cause)
        {
            <span class="cause">@cause</span>
            <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">·</span>
        }
        <Moment At="Entry.At" Now="Now" />
    </span>
</article>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public FeedEntry Entry { get; set; } = default!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public FeedKind Kind { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public DateTimeOffset Now { get; set; } = DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;

    [Parameter] public bool ShowRegister { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>
    /// Why a game went dark, where the feed knows — and nothing at all on the other two registers.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>
    /// A cause is a measurement: the socket was refused, or the name did not resolve, or nothing
    /// came back before the timeout. It belongs on the row because it is not what the heading says.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The discovery feed's detail is the opposite case — "found via REFERRAL from M*U*S*H ·
    /// answered MSSP on first probe · PennMUSH 1.8.8p0" is three facts that are all on the game's
    /// own page, printed under a heading that already says the game is new. It is dropped here, not
    /// hidden: the page it belongs on is one click away and states each of them with its provenance.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    private string? Cause => Kind is FeedKind.WentDark ? Entry.Detail : null;

    private string Register => Kind switch
    {
        FeedKind.WentDark => "unlit",
        FeedKind.CameBack => "returned",
        _ => "neutral",
    };

    private string Heading => Kind switch
    {
        FeedKind.WentDark => "went dark",
        FeedKind.CameBack => "came back",
        _ => "newly discovered",
    };
}
